From: prospero-win orbis <noreply@localhost>
Subject: ntdll: keep a hot host page whole beside a read-only page

Patch 0974 single-steps writes to a writable page whose host page holds a
read-only one. After 256 such writes on one host page it reported the next
write as a write to the read-only page, so that an emulator protecting its
recompiled code would drop that protection. An ordinary program gets a fault
it never caused: the Unofficial Patch of Vampire: The Masquerade - Bloodlines
maps a module itself, makes one of its pages read-only and goes on filling the
page before it, and its loader.dll took the reported write for an access
violation and failed to load ("Failed to load the loader DLL: No access to
memory location"). The PC's Wine with WINE_HOST_PAGE_SIZE=16384 does the same
and loads the DLL once the report is gone.

Instead, the hot host page gets the union of its pages' protections, as before
0974, and the write runs again without a step, as patch 0986 upstream does for
accesses beside an inaccessible page. The host page is split again when a
protection in it changes (mprotect_range recomputes it), and its count starts
again. Until then a write to the read-only page itself is not seen, as before
0974: a program that watches its own read-only pages through faults, beside
pages it writes this often, no longer gets that fault.
---
--- a/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
@@ -5227,9 +5227,9 @@ static struct { const char *page; unsigned int steps; } subpage_heat[64];
  *
  * Counts a write stepped over in a host page because a page in it is read-only.
  * Once the host page has had SUBPAGE_HOT_STEPS of them, returns that read-only
- * page, to report the write there instead; NULL to step this one. The count
- * starts again after a report, so a program that keeps the page read-only gets
- * one report per SUBPAGE_HOT_STEPS writes and the rest are stepped, not a loop.
+ * page: the caller then keeps the host page whole instead of stepping; NULL to
+ * step this one. The count starts again then, for when the host page is split
+ * again by a protection change.
  */
 static char *subpage_hot_write( char *page )
 {
@@ -5323,17 +5323,21 @@ NTSTATUS virtual_handle_fault( struct thread_data *data, EXCEPTION_RECORD *rec,
          * again in virtual_subpage_step_done when the single step traps. */
         int need = err == EXCEPTION_WRITE_FAULT ? PROT_WRITE : err == EXCEPTION_EXECUTE_FAULT ? PROT_EXEC : PROT_READ;
         int prot = get_unix_prot( get_page_vprot( addr ) );
-        char *protected_page;
 
         if (!(prot & need) || (get_host_page_unix_prot( page, NULL, NULL, 0, 0 ) & need))
             ;
-        else if (err == EXCEPTION_WRITE_FAULT && (protected_page = subpage_hot_write( page )))
-        {
-            /* Writes beside a read-only page come too often to step each one: report a
-             * write to that page instead, as if this one had touched it. A program that
-             * protects pages to learn when they change (an emulator's recompiled code)
-             * then drops its protection, and the host page is writable again. */
-            rec->ExceptionInformation[1] = (ULONG_PTR)protected_page;
+        else if (err == EXCEPTION_WRITE_FAULT && subpage_hot_write( page ))
+        {
+            /* Writes beside a read-only page come too often to step each one. Give the
+             * host page the union of its pages' protections, as before the split, and
+             * let this write run again unstepped. A fault reported on the read-only page
+             * instead would be one the program never caused: a loader filling the page
+             * next to a section it made read-only takes it for a real access violation.
+             * The host page is split again when a protection in it changes, since that
+             * goes through mprotect_range. Until then a write to the read-only page
+             * itself is not seen, as before the split. */
+            if (!mprotect_exec( page, host_page_size, get_unix_prot( get_host_page_vprot( page ) ) ))
+                ret = STATUS_SUCCESS;
         }
         else if (!mprotect_exec( page, host_page_size, get_unix_prot( get_host_page_vprot( page ) ) ))
         {
